 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Standard NeRF training complexity, extended by depth loss computation. Total steps are fixed at 500,000 [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Standard NeRF memory usage, plus storage for depth maps and dropout masks.
-- **Performance:** Improves geometric accuracy in scenes with ambiguous geometry by leveraging depth priors [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Robustness to sparse views; better depth consistency.
-- **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth or reliable depth estimation; sensitive to depth loss weight $\lambda$ [S1].
-- **When to Use:** Sparse-view reconstruction, scenes with textureless regions, or when geometric accuracy is prioritized over photometric fidelity.
-- **When Not to Use:** Dense-view scenarios where standard NeRF suffices; scenes without reliable depth priors.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes 500,000 steps [S1]. The exact time per step depends on hardware and scene complexity, but the addition of depth loss computation adds minimal overhead compared to RGB loss.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard NeRF memory usage plus storage for ground truth depth maps if used during training [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves geometric accuracy and training stability through depth supervision. The dropout regularization helps prevent overfitting [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth maps for training. Performance may vary across scenes, necessitating scene-specific hyperparameter tuning (e.g., scene 710) [S1]. Reproducing results from other works (like DS-NeRF) may be difficult due to missing configuration files or undocumented architectural modifications [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the integration of depth guidance into NeRF training, as detailed in "Depth-Guided NeRF Training via Earth Mover’s Distance" [S1]. The method builds on the original NeRF architecture [Parent](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) and incorporates techniques from DS-NeRF for depth loss integration [S2]. It is used in applications requiring high-fidelity 3D reconstruction from limited or noisy pose data, such as in the SPARF framework [S2].
+This configuration was introduced in the context of Depth-Guided NeRF training using Earth Mover’s Distance [S1]. It builds upon the original NeRF architecture [Parent](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) and incorporates insights from DS-NeRF regarding depth loss integration [S2]. The method is particularly relevant for applications where accurate 3D geometry is crucial, such as in ScanNet and DTU datasets [S1, S2].
 
